

Engineering edition it is. Here’s a solid, “systems-first” layout for the QUENNE Orbital Star Ring Smart City (ring habitat) that an engineer can immediately critique, simulate, and iterate.

1) Core architecture (what the city is made of)

A. Central Hub (Non-rotating “Zero-G Core”)

Purpose: docking, freight, control, data center, attitude control.
   •   Docking spine: visiting vehicles + QTSP-style cargo/crew modules
   •   Airlocks & quarantine: medical/biocontainment entry zone
   •   Power conditioning + batteries: load balancing for the whole habitat
   •   Data center / mission ops: city OS, digital twin, traffic, security
   •   Fuel & prop storage (segregated): away from crew volume
   •   Thermal radiators: usually mounted here (best for large heat rejection)

B. Rotating Ring (Artificial-G “Living & Working City”)

Purpose: humans live/work under partial or near-Earth gravity.
   •   Divided into sectors (dwelling, hospital, market, labs, etc.)
   •   Each sector has local utilities (air, water loops, power distribution)
   •   Pressure hull with internal decks (multi-level city block design)

C. Spokes (Transition Zone)

Purpose: connect core to ring; carry utilities; manage rotation interface.
   •   Elevator / tram shafts + emergency ladders
   •   Rotary joints for power/data/fluid transfer (critical engineering node)
   •   Shock isolation between ring vibrations and core

