3) The big engineering constraints (the “non-negotiables”)

A) Artificial gravity (rotation)

Key rule: people tolerate rotation better when rpm is low and radius is larger.
   •   Choose a ring radius that keeps rpm comfortable
   •   Build a gravity gradient plan: lower decks slightly lower-g, upper decks slightly higher-g (design around it)

B) Radiation protection
   •   Storm shelters: concentrated shielding (water tanks, supplies, composite shielding)
   •   Distributed shielding: wall layers + equipment as “mass shielding”
   •   Put sleeping quarters closer to shielded ribs (humans spend most time there)

C) Thermal control (heat is the enemy)
   •   Data centers, industry, and power electronics must dump heat to radiators
   •   Heat zoning: hot modules closer to core radiator plumbing routes
   •   Waste heat reuse: warm agriculture, water heating, habitat comfort

D) Pressure safety & compartmentalization

Make the ring a set of sealed neighborhoods:
   •   Automatic bulkheads that close on pressure drop
   •   Independent air loops per sector (limit smoke/contaminants spread)

E) Logistics & transport
   •   Ring transit loop: people + cargo tram (clockwise/counterclockwise redundancy)
   •   Spoke freight elevators: heavy cargo prioritized on fewer dedicated spokes
   •   Core-to-ring interface is the “port”—design it like an airport + seaport combined

